  "blocking_issues": [],
  "scope": "Independent offline visual review of photographic references, source renders, fixed-distance evidence, and export appearance comparison. No native editor work performed.",
  "method": "Viewed the listed photographic references and 38 preview images directly, including original distant frames and separately labelled magnified pairs. Read distance cameras/metrics and final QA/comparison receipts. Independently rehashed all 33 distance PNGs, their bound source/textures, final eight FBXs, comparison inputs/outputs and the files below. No modelling or native editor operation was performed by this reviewer.",
  "observations": [
    {
      "topic": "Young strawberry plants",
      "finding": "A/B/C show separate sparse young plants, serrated trifoliate leaflets with restrained veins and varied poses/sizes; no visible fruit, flowers or continuous near-range canopy block."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Raised bed and film",
      "finding": "Rounded shoulders, sloped dark matte film, slightly varied surface and continuous soil-covered side edges replace the original rigid-looking bed. In supported source renders the skirt is hidden and the bed sits on the plane."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Planting-stage reference",
      "finding": "The Huelva comparison supports the bed/film morphology; UC photographs support the leafier young-plug stage. The comparison is a morphology reference, not an exposure/color match or an exact reconstruction."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Joins and slope",
      "finding": "The 10-row 20 m regular chains on 3% slope appear continuous with no visible tile steps, open gaps, repeated end walls or broken orientation. A/B/C strip views show continuous material and silhouette through their joins."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Lowest LOD",
      "finding": "Lowest models remain dark film stripes with restrained green dots, with no bright green band or distant pale drift. Measurements show close coverage/color agreement with detailed models."
    },
    {
      "topic": "End cap joint",
      "finding": "The final fresh-FBX A-plus-cap close view at 2.285 m shows continuous geometric contact and no conspicuous material/color boundary. The cap rolls down into a soil-covered end. The separate geometric QA warning about UV phase is retained below."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Export appearance",
      "finding": "Final source and fresh-FBX plant close images appear equivalent; the prior tiny-petiole shading regression is resolved. The comparison receipt measures 115 of 1,400,000 pixels above a two-byte maximum-channel difference (0.008214%). Final eight-asset QA reports minimum authored corner-normal dot 0.9999996326."
    }
  ],
  "nonblocking_issues": [
    {
      "topic": "Cap texture phase",
      "detail": "Detailed A/B/C-to-cap film UV phase differs by up to about 0.393 U, as recorded by geometry QA. No conspicuous material boundary appears in the A-plus-cap close view or mixed-row field views. This is not mathematically seamless UV mapping; a later higher-contrast mulch texture could expose it. B/C have no separate dedicated cap-close image."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Photographic likeness",
      "detail": "The asset resolves the former canopy-block appearance and follows the selected planting-stage morphology, while the synthetic film, dirt and vegetation remain simpler, smoother and more regular than the photographs."
    }
  ],
  "limits": [
    "The surface remains recognizably procedural: fine film wrinkles, dirt/clods and plant microdetail are simpler and more regular than the photographs. This is not a photorealistic scan.",
    "The render is darker and more uniform than the sunlit Huelva photo. Camera exposure, illumination, soil condition and plant stage differ; the model intentionally uses leafy plugs rather than Huelva's largely bare crowns.",
    "Photographs supply visual direction only. Brief dimensions were not inferred as measurements from those photos.",
    "Results are offline Blender source previews. Native Unreal import, material configuration, placement, Nanite/HLOD, streaming, lighting and persistence have not been validated.",
    "At 4000 m the entire block covers about 6.45 equivalent opaque pixels at the declared 1920x1080 and 110-degree horizontal FOV. Individual beds/plants are below useful raster separation; labelled magnified crops do not create extra detail.",
    "The 1 m single views intentionally crop for detail. Distant views retain fixed FOV/resolution and do not auto-frame.",
    "Ground-masked alpha comparisons concern the visible bed geometry, excluding buried skirts and cast shadows. Since mulch occupies most pixels, whole-bed color/area agreement does not prove preservation of individual leaves.",
    "The 3% slope evidence uses a coherent plane and endpoint-following placement. It does not prove contact on arbitrary uneven native terrain."
  ],
